A scissor that is just the whole screen doesn't cut horizontally in an
expanded aspect ratio (warecomp STATUS.md round 8).

With Expand, a scissor without extended origins is scaled about the
centre, so the game's full-screen scissor (0-320) became the middle 4:3
area and cut everything drawn past the screen's sides. Worms Armageddon
draws its level past them for widescreen (name tags, sprites, water were
cut at the old 4:3 edges). Two places:

- framebuffer renderer: a draw whose scissor spans the whole colour image
  width and has no origins gets the whole wide target horizontally;
- S2DEX bg1Cyc, which cuts a background frame to the scissor as the
  microcode does: not horizontally for such a scissor.

Smaller scissors, and scissors with origins (gEXSetScissor), are as before.

--- a/src/gbi/rt64_gbi_s2dex.cpp
+++ b/src/gbi/rt64_gbi_s2dex.cpp
@@ -178,6 +178,15 @@
             int32_t scissorY0 = scissorRect.uly;
             int32_t scissorX1 = scissorRect.lrx;
             int32_t scissorY1 = scissorRect.lry;
+
+            // A scissor spanning the whole colour image is just the screen: don't cut the
+            // frame horizontally there, so that with an expanded aspect ratio a background
+            // reaching past the screen's sides shows like every other draw does.
+            if ((scissorX0 <= 0) && (scissorX1 >= (int32_t(rdp->colorImage.width) << 2))) {
+                scissorX0 = std::min<int32_t>(scissorX0, frameX0);
+                scissorX1 = std::max<int32_t>(scissorX1, frameX0 + frameW);
+            }
+
             int16_t pixX0 = int16_t(std::max(scissorX0 - frameX0, 0));
             int16_t pixY0 = int16_t(std::max(scissorY0 - frameY0, 0));
             int16_t pixX1 = int16_t(std::max(frameW - scissorX1 + frameX0, 0));
